WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.185
Module five:

2
00:00:01.185 --> 00:00:02.230
DeFi protocols.

3
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:07.033
Collateralized loans, sources of yield, staking, bridges and oracles,

4
00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:11.558
and how to assess a protocol's risk before putting money into it.

5
00:00:12.558 --> 00:00:16.891
A protocol does not know who you are, so trust is replaced by collateral.

6
00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:20.036
You deposit coins worth more than you want to borrow.

7
00:00:20.036 --> 00:00:21.935
Three numbers decide everything:

8
00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:26.268
how much you can borrow, the liquidation threshold and the loan's health.

9
00:00:26.268 --> 00:00:28.998
While health is above one, everything is fine.

10
00:00:29.998 --> 00:00:35.123
In the lesson you can drop the collateral price and see when the loan's health falls

11
00:00:35.123 --> 00:00:35.733
below one.

12
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:40.858
Then any liquidator repays part of the debt and takes the collateral with a bonus of

13
00:00:40.858 --> 00:00:42.078
five to ten percent.

14
00:00:42.078 --> 00:00:45.190
Bots do this in seconds, without calls or warnings.

15
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.004
Protocols display yields in big numbers.

16
00:00:49.004 --> 00:00:50.904
The one useful question is:

17
00:00:50.904 --> 00:00:51.748
who pays it?

18
00:00:51.748 --> 00:00:54.281
In a lending deposit, the borrowers.

19
00:00:54.281 --> 00:00:58.221
In pools, the traders, in exchange for impermanent loss.

20
00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:00.894
In staking, the issuance of new coins.

21
00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.498
In farming, the protocol's own token.

22
00:01:03.498 --> 00:01:06.734
Or you yourself, if the source is not visible.

23
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:11.706
If the network's issuance is three percent a year and you get three,

24
00:01:11.706 --> 00:01:14.043
you are simply keeping up with dilution.

25
00:01:14.043 --> 00:01:17.723
Farming at 200 percent is usually paid in the protocol's token,

26
00:01:17.723 --> 00:01:22.279
which is printed and sold, and its price falls faster than the income accrues.

27
00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:27.011
And a deposit with a boosted rate sometimes turns out to be a structured product:

28
00:01:27.011 --> 00:01:29.406
you are selling risk for a small premium.

29
00:01:30.406 --> 00:01:35.324
In proof-of-stake networks, validators put up a stake and receive a reward.

30
00:01:35.324 --> 00:01:36.701
The risks of staking:

31
00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:40.176
penalties for validator mistakes, a withdrawal queue,

32
00:01:40.176 --> 00:01:44.045
and the reliability of whoever you trusted with your coins.

33
00:01:44.045 --> 00:01:46.537
Liquid staking gives you an IOU token,

34
00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:49.750
but it is worth only what the market pays for it:

35
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:53.422
in June 2022 stETH traded below ether for several weeks.

36
00:01:54.422 --> 00:01:56.524
Networks cannot see each other.

37
00:01:56.524 --> 00:02:01.882
A bridge takes a coin into a vault on one network and issues a wrapped token on

38
00:02:01.882 --> 00:02:02.425
another.

39
00:02:02.425 --> 00:02:07.375
The IOU is valuable while the vault is intact, and vaults hold huge sums.

40
00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:12.191
In 2022 about 320 million dollars were stolen from the Wormhole bridge,

41
00:02:12.191 --> 00:02:15.446
and more than 600 million from the Ronin bridge.

42
00:02:16.446 --> 00:02:19.489
An oracle tells a contract a price from outside.

43
00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:22.468
If the price is easy to move, it will be moved.

44
00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:26.906
In October 2022 more than 100 million were drained from Mango Markets:

45
00:02:26.906 --> 00:02:29.568
the price of an illiquid token was pumped,

46
00:02:29.568 --> 00:02:32.484
the protocol believed the inflated collateral,

47
00:02:32.484 --> 00:02:35.400
and almost everything was borrowed against it.

48
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.098
Protection:

49
00:02:36.098 --> 00:02:40.662
several price sources, a time average and limits on illiquid collateral.

50
00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:45.363
Money in a deposit often sits on several layers at once:

51
00:02:45.363 --> 00:02:50.319
the issuer's stablecoin, a bridge, a second layer, the protocol, an oracle,

52
00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:51.046
a multisig.

53
00:02:51.046 --> 00:02:54.020
Each layer has its own risk, and they add up.

54
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.051
An audit lowers the chance of a bug but does not rule it out:

55
00:02:58.051 --> 00:03:00.694
many hacked protocols had passed audits.

56
00:03:01.694 --> 00:03:03.689
Five questions before depositing.

57
00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:07.680
How many years has the protocol run with a large sum and no hacks?

58
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.186
Who can change the code, and how many keys does that take?

59
00:03:11.186 --> 00:03:13.000
Where do the prices come from?

60
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.661
How many layers does the money pass through?

61
00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:17.051
And who pays the yield?

62
00:03:17.051 --> 00:03:20.558
A protocol's size tells you its size, not its reliability.

63
00:03:21.558 --> 00:03:22.609
The main points.

64
00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:25.894
Loans rest on collateral worth more than the debt,

65
00:03:25.894 --> 00:03:30.821
and when it falls the contract itself sells the collateral to a liquidator.

66
00:03:30.821 --> 00:03:32.398
Every yield has a payer.

67
00:03:32.398 --> 00:03:37.062
And the risks of the network, bridge, contract, oracle and keys add up.
